I would trim the input, split on one or more whitespace characters so repeated spaces do not produce empty words, and join the words in reverse:

```java
public static String reverseWords(String s) {
    if (s == null) return null;
    String trimmed = s.trim();
    if (trimmed.isEmpty()) return "";
    String[] words = trimmed.split("\\s+");
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(trimmed.length());
    for (int i = words.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        sb.append(words[i]);
        if (i > 0) sb.append(' ');
    }
    return sb.toString();
}
```

This is O(n) time and O(n) space in the length of the string, since the split array and the builder each hold the characters once. Tests I would write: a normal sentence, a single word, empty and whitespace-only strings, null, leading, trailing and multiple internal spaces, tabs, and punctuation attached to words. An in-place version on a char array reverses the whole array and then reverses each word, which uses O(1) extra space, though Java strings are immutable so you need the array copy anyway.

I would use two hash sets: one for values seen and one for duplicates:

```java
public static Set<Integer> findDuplicates(int[] nums) {
    Set<Integer> seen = new HashSet<>();
    Set<Integer> dupes = new LinkedHashSet<>();
    for (int n : nums) {
        if (!seen.add(n)) {
            dupes.add(n);
        }
    }
    return dupes;
}
```

Set.add returns false when the value is already present, so each element is processed once: O(n) average time and O(n) extra space. LinkedHashSet keeps duplicates in the order they were first detected, which makes test assertions predictable. Without extra memory I can sort the array first, in place, and compare neighbours, which is O(n log n) time and O(1) extra space apart from the sort itself, but it modifies the input. For a stream too large for memory, I would partition values by hash into files and process each partition separately, or accept approximate answers with a probabilistic structure such as a Bloom filter if false positives are acceptable.

A stack is the natural fit: push each opening bracket, and for each closing bracket check that the top of the stack is the matching opener:

```java
public static boolean isBalanced(String s) {
    Deque<Character> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
    for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
        switch (c) {
            case '(', '[', '{' -> stack.push(c);
            case ')' -> { if (stack.isEmpty() || stack.pop() != '(') return false; }
            case ']' -> { if (stack.isEmpty() || stack.pop() != '[') return false; }
            case '}' -> { if (stack.isEmpty() || stack.pop() != '{') return false; }
            default -> { }
        }
    }
    return stack.isEmpty();
}
```

It runs in O(n) time and O(n) space in the worst case, when all characters are openers. The final isEmpty check catches unclosed openers such as '((', and the isEmpty check before pop catches a closer with nothing open, such as ')'. Deque with ArrayDeque is preferred over java.util.Stack because Stack extends Vector, is synchronised and is a legacy class. Tests: empty string, a single pair of each type, nested and sequential pairs, wrong order like '(]', interleaved like '([)]', only openers, only closers, and non-bracket characters which this version ignores.

I would stream the file line by line so memory stays bounded, count errors per service in a map, then sort the counts:

```java
public static List<Map.Entry<String, Long>> topErrorServices(Path log, int k) throws IOException {
    Map<String, Long> counts = new HashMap<>();
    try (Stream<String> lines = Files.lines(log)) {
        lines.map(line -> line.split("\\s+", 4))
             .filter(parts -> parts.length >= 3 && parts[1].equals("ERROR"))
             .forEach(parts -> counts.merge(parts[2], 1L, Long::sum));
    }
    return counts.entrySet().stream()
        .sorted(Map.Entry.<String, Long>comparingByValue().reversed()
            .thenComparing(Map.Entry.comparingByKey()))
        .limit(k)
        .toList();
}
```

Splitting with a limit of four keeps the message intact and avoids extra work. Lines with fewer than three fields are skipped; in a real tool I would count and report malformed lines rather than ignore them silently. Time is O(n) to read the lines plus O(s log s) to sort s distinct services, which is small; a min-heap of size k would make the selection O(s log k). Memory is O(s), independent of file size, because Files.lines reads lazily, so a 20 GB file works as long as the number of services is reasonable. The tie-break by service name makes the result deterministic for tests.

I would keep it small and make the retryable exception type explicit so real failures are not swallowed:

```java
public static <T> T retry(Supplier<T> action, int maxAttempts, Duration delay,
                          Class<? extends RuntimeException> retryOn) {
    if (maxAttempts < 1) throw new IllegalArgumentException("maxAttempts must be >= 1");
    RuntimeException last = null;
    for (int attempt = 1; attempt <= maxAttempts; attempt++) {
        try {
            return action.get();
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            if (!retryOn.isInstance(e)) throw e;
            last = e;
            if (attempt < maxAttempts) {
                try {
                    Thread.sleep(delay.toMillis());
                } catch (InterruptedException ie) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                    throw new IllegalStateException("Interrupted while retrying", ie);
                }
            }
        }
    }
    throw last;
}
```

It restores the interrupt flag rather than swallowing InterruptedException, and rethrows the last cause so the failure stays diagnosable. Exponential backoff multiplies the delay each attempt up to a cap, and jitter randomises it so many clients do not retry in lockstep. To unit test it without real sleeps I would inject a sleeper or Clock abstraction and use a fake. In tests, I would use such a helper for polling legitimately eventual operations, like waiting for an async job, but not to rerun whole failing tests or assertions, because that hides flakiness and real intermittent product bugs.

Two passes are simplest: count characters, then find the first with a count of one, using a LinkedHashMap or a second scan over the string:

```java
public static Optional<Character> firstUnique(String s) {
    Map<Character, Integer> counts = new HashMap<>();
    for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
        counts.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);
    }
    for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
        if (counts.get(c) == 1) return Optional.of(c);
    }
    return Optional.empty();
}
```

This is O(n) time and O(k) space where k is the number of distinct characters. A single pass is possible with a LinkedHashMap of counts and then scanning the map, which is O(k) rather than O(n) for the second phase. Java chars are UTF-16 code units, so characters outside the Basic Multilingual Plane, such as many emoji, are two chars; to handle them correctly I would iterate s.codePoints() and use Integer code points as keys. Tests: empty string, all repeated, unique at start, middle and end, case sensitivity, and a string with emoji.
